WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 254: Nikola Tesla: The Master of Lightning, the AC Induction Motor, and the Wireless Vision

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:16.080
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the ethereal realm of high frequency, wireless energy, and artificial lightning.

4
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.960
[Rebecca] The life of the brilliant, tragic visionary who invented the alternating current system that powers planet Earth.

5
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Nikola Tesla.

6
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:43.280
[George] According to family legend, Nikola Tesla was born at the stroke of midnight during a terrifying summer electrical storm in July 1856 in the village of Smiljen, on the mountainous Croatian military frontier of the Austrian Empire.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:50.800
[George] As lightning crashed over the house, the terrified midwife wrung her hands and declared the infant to be a child of darkness.

8
00:00:50.800 --> 00:01:00.480
[George] His mother Georgina Mandik, an illiterate but gifted inventor of household tools, replied with fierce conviction, no, he will be a child of light.

9
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:12.720
[George] From early childhood, Tesla possessed a mind of astonishing, photographic vitality, accompanied by intense, blinding flashes of light that appeared before his eyes whenever an idea formed.

10
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:24.960
[George] He could perform complex integral calculus entirely in his head, visualize machines in three dimensions down to the smallest bolt without drawing blueprints, and memorize entire books word for word.

11
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.080
[George] While studying engineering at the Joanneum Polytechnic School in Graz, Austria.

12
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:45.600
[George] Tesla watched a sputtering gram dynamo sparking violently on its direct current commutator, blurting out to his physics professor that direct current was an inefficient, mechanical dead end, and that an alternating current motor could run without sparks or friction.

13
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.400
[George] The professor dismissed his idea as an impossible perpetual motion fantasy.

14
00:01:50.400 --> 00:02:02.920
[George] In February 1882, while walking through the city park in Budapest at sunset reciting Goethe's Faust, an electrifying revelation hit Tesla, drawing a diagram in the dirt with a walking stick.

15
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:10.840
[George] Tesla conceived the rotating magnetic field, the foundational principle of modern polyphase electrical machinery.

16
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:22.840
[George] Tesla's extraordinary photographic memory allowed him to visualize complex mechanical engines in operation, detecting microware on imaginary gear teeth before a single metal casting was made.

17
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:34.280
[George] His epiphany in Budapest while quoting Goethe's Faust revealed the profound poetic nature of his scientific genius, reconciling classical literature with electromagnetic field theory.

18
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:51.000
[George] In June 1884, 28-year-old Nicola Tesla arrived in New York Harbor with four cents in his pocket, a book of his own poetry, and a glowing letter of recommendation from Charles Bachelor, Edison's European manager, addressed to Thomas Alva Edison.

19
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.280
[George] The letter contained a legendary sentence I know two great men and you are one of them, the other is this young man.

20
00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:04.760
[George] Edison hired Tesla immediately to repair direct current dynamos aboard the ocean liner SS Oregon.

21
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.520
[George] But the intellectual contrast between the two men was total.

22
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.920
[George] Edison was an unschooled, empirical pragmatist who solved problems through relentless trial and error.

23
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:21.160
[George] Tesla was a refined European theoretician steeped in classical poetry and mathematical precision.

24
00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:32.760
[George] When Tesla proposed redesigning Edison's inefficient direct current dynamos to drastically boost performance, Edison allegedly promised him a bonus of $50,000 if he succeeded.

25
00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:40.040
[George] Tesla labored 18 hours a day for months, redesigning the dynamos and securing valuable patents for the company.

26
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] When Tesla asked for his promised reward, Edison laughed coldly and dismissed the claim.

27
00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.800
[George] Tesla.

28
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.520
[George] You don't understand our American humor.

29
00:03:49.520 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] Deeply disgusted by what he viewed as a dishonorable betrayal, Tesla resigned on the spot, walking into the bitter winter of 1886 to dig ditches in New York streets for $2 a day to avoid starvation.

30
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.040
[George] Tesla's immigration to the United States was marked by tragedy and triumph.

31
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:14.880
[George] Robbed of his steamship ticket and baggage in Paris, he arrived in Manhattan with nothing but his dreams and mathematical notebooks.

32
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:27.840
[George] The bitter ditch-digging winter of 1886 tested Tesla's spirit, yet he refused to surrender his belief in alternating current, using his evening hours to sketch improved polyphase motor designs.

33
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:37.520
[George] In 1887, impressed by Tesla's brilliance, a group of investors provided funding to establish the Tesla Electric Company on South Fifth Avenue.

34
00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:45.280
[George] Within months, Tesla patented his masterwork, the polyphase alternating current induction motor and transformer system.

35
00:04:45.280 --> 00:05:01.840
[George] In May 1888, Tesla delivered his historic lecture before the American Institute of Electrical Engineers, demonstrating an electric motor that operated smoothly without brushes, commutators, or sparks by riding an invisible wave of rotating magnetic flux.

36
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:10.560
[George] In the audience sat Pittsburgh industrialist George Westinghouse, who instantly recognized that Tesla held the key to the electrical future.

37
00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:20.480
[George] Westinghouse purchased Tesla's patents for $60,000 plus a generous royalty of $2.50 per horsepower of electrical power generated.

38
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.880
[George] The alliance ignited the furious war of the currents against Edison and J.P.

39
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.720
[George] Morgan's General Electric.

40
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:45.560
[George] In 1893, Westinghouse and Tesla triumphed decisively, winning the contract to illuminate the world's Columbian exposition in Chicago with a quarter million alternating current lamps, turning the fairgrounds into a glowing white city that dazzled 27 million international visitors.

41
00:05:45.560 --> 00:06:01.560
[George] When Westinghouse later faced bankruptcy due to Wall Street takeovers and begged Tesla for relief, Tesla tore up his multimillion dollar royalty contract in a selfless gesture of friendship, forfeiting a personal fortune that would have made him the wealthiest man on earth.

42
00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:13.480
[George] The demonstration before the American Institute of Electrical Engineers was an intellectual earthquake, proving that alternating current could drive heavy machinery without sparking commutators or friction.

43
00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:25.240
[George] Tesla's selfless tearing up of his royalty contract saved George Westinghouse from financial ruin, cementing a lifelong fraternal friendship between the industrialist and the Serbian inventor.

44
00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] Since childhood, Tesla had dreamed of harnessing the colossal mechanical energy of the thundering waterfalls he had read about in American schoolbooks.

45
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:44.360
[George] In 1893, an international commission headed by Lord Kelvin selected the Tesla polyphase alternating current system to harness the power of Niagara Falls.

46
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:59.320
[George] Constructing the Edward Dean Adams Power Plant, Westinghouse engineers installed three colossal 5,000 horsepower Tesla two-phase generators driven by massive water turbines set in deep bedrock shafts beneath the roaring cataract.

47
00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] On the midnight of November 16, 1896.

48
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.240
[George] The master switch was thrown.

49
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:18.960
[George] Instantly, 15,000 horsepower of clean, hydroelectric alternating currents surged 26 miles down high-voltage transmission lines into the city of Buffalo, powering street railways and incandescent factories.

50
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.640
[George] Within years, Niagara electricity reached New York City 400 miles away.

51
00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] The Niagara plant was an epic milestone in human civilization.

52
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:43.600
[George] It proved that clean energy from rushing water could be transformed into high-voltage electricity and transmitted across immense continental distances to power human cities, validating Tesla's revolutionary electrical theories beyond all doubt.

53
00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:56.160
[George] The engineering feat at Niagara Falls required excavating massive subterranean wheelpits through solid limestone, installing water turbines that rotated giant 5,000 horsepower generators.

54
00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:08.640
[George] When the power surged into Buffalo, electric streetcars began running on Niagara Current, proving to the world that long-distance high-voltage transmission was the foundation of the modern electrified economy.

55
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:21.040
[George] Moving beyond low-frequency power grids, Tesla became obsessed with the mysterious phenomena of high-frequency and high-voltage electricity, inventing the resonant transformer known as the Tesla coil.

56
00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:38.720
[George] In May 1899, Tesla moved his research to a remote wooden laboratory on the high plateau of Colorado Springs, constructing a colossal magnifying transmitter topped by an 80-foot wooden tower and a three-foot copper sphere, powering the transmitter with high-voltage currents.

57
00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:54.480
[George] Tesla created artificial lightning bolts over 135 feet in length, producing deafening thunderclaps heard 20 miles away in Cripple Creek and lighting 200 wireless incandescent lamps planted directly in the dirt seven miles from the laboratory.

58
00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:03.800
[George] Tesla claimed to have measured terrestrial stationary electrical waves, proving that the Earth itself was a resonant conductor that could be vibrated electrically.

59
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:11.880
[George] In July 1899, while monitoring sensitive electrical receivers in the silence of the night, Tesla detected rhythmic.

60
00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:23.000
[George] Repetitive clicking signals that he believed were interplanetary communications from intelligent civilizations on Mars, igniting global headlines and capturing the Victorian imagination.

61
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:27.000
[George] The Colorado Springs Laboratory was a site of cosmic wonder.

62
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:36.680
[George] The giant primary coil vibrated the ground for miles around, causing sparks to jump from horses' hooves and butterflies to glow with blue corona discharge.

63
00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:48.120
[George] Tesla's belief that he had intercepted extraterrestrial signals from Mars reflected his profound conviction that human intelligence was not alone in the vast electromagnetic expanse of the universe.

64
00:09:48.120 --> 00:10:04.760
[George] Returning to New York in 1900, Tesla envisioned a grand world wireless system that would combine global radio broadcasting, telegraphy, telephony, facsimile transmission, and wireless electrical power distribution into a single planetary network.

65
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:08.600
[George] Securing 150,000 from financier J.

66
00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:08.760
[George] P.

67
00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.640
[George] Morgan, Tesla purchased 200 acres at Shoreham on Long Island.

68
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:28.760
[George] Commissioning legendary architect Stanford White to design the brick laboratory and a colossal 187 foot octagonal wooden tower capped by a 55 ton steel hemispherical dome, anchored deep into the Earth by a subterranean shaft.

69
00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:35.960
[George] Known as Wardencliffe, the facility was designed to beam wireless power and intelligence across the Atlantic Ocean.

70
00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:47.200
[George] But in December 1901, Guglielmo Marconi used 17 of Tesla's basic radio patents to transmit the Morse code letter S across the Atlantic using a simple transmitter.

71
00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:57.280
[George] Realizing that Tesla was pursuing wireless power rather than a commercial radio monopoly, Morgan withdrew his financial backing and refused further loans.

72
00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:09.280
[George] Bankrupted, heartbroken, and unable to pay his debts, Tesla watched in despair as Wardencliffe was abandoned, and in 1917 the magnificent tower was demolished for scrap metal.

73
00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:22.320
[George] Wardencliffe was intended as the crown jewel of human communication, capable of transmitting world news, music, private stock quotes, and marine navigation signals across oceans without a single wire.

74
00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:33.920
[George] The demolition of the Wardencliffe Tower during World War I shattered Tesla's spirit, leaving the visionary inventor buried in crushing debts and forced to abandon his greatest scientific dream.

75
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:48.240
[George] In his final decades, Nicola Tesla lived as an ascetic, reclusive philosopher in Suite 30 327 of the Hotel New Yorker, feeding flocks of white pigeons in Bryant Park and nursing injured birds on his window ledge.

76
00:11:48.240 --> 00:12:09.760
[George] While sensational newspapers labeled him an eccentric mad scientist chasing death rays and cosmic rays, Tesla continued to conceptualize revolutionary technologies, inventing the bladeless turbine, pioneering radio guided robotic boats, and predicting modern smartphones, radar, and the Internet decades before their creation.

77
00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:17.920
[George] In 1943, at age 86, Tesla died peacefully in his sleep, penniless and alone in his hotel room.

78
00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:28.960
[George] Months after his death, the United States Supreme Court overturned Marconi's radio patents, officially ruling that Nicola Tesla was the true original inventor of radio.

79
00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:44.120
[George] Today, every alternating current motor, every high voltage power line, every hydroelectric dam, and every wireless device on Earth traces its lineage back to the fertile imagination of the Serbian boy born in a midnight thunderstorm.

80
00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:53.320
[George] Nikola Tesla remains the ultimate patron saint of science, the man who illuminated a planet and dreamed of giving humanity the power of the stars.

81
00:12:53.320 --> 00:13:04.760
[George] In his suite at the Hotel New Yorker, Tesla spent his evenings feeding pigeons in Bryant Park, claiming a mystical spiritual communion with a pure white dove that visited his window sill.

82
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:16.120
[George] The Supreme Court's posthumous recognition of Tesla's priority in radio technology restored his rightful place in history as the undisputed father of wireless telecommunications.

83
00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.800
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

